Falsifies `kernel::arch::x86_64::ist::tests::an_nmi_finds_its_per_cpu_block_whatever_the_gs_base_held`.

Architecture: x86_64

The defect: `isr_paranoid` never writes the kernel's GS base, so the NMI handler runs with whatever
`IA32_GS_BASE` held when the NMI arrived, which in the windows around `swapgs` is the user's. The
saved CS says ring 0 there, so a CS-guarded `swapgs` would not fire either; this is that case.

Expected to fail: "the NMI handler read its per-CPU pointer through the decoy GS base".
Replayed red on patagonia, 2026-10-13 UTC, `script/test --arch x86_64 --no-host --test an_nmi_finds_its_per_cpu_block_whatever_the_gs_base_held`
(QEMU q35 under PVH, two cores); green with the fix.

diff --git a/kernel/src/arch/x86_64/trap.s b/kernel/src/arch/x86_64/trap.s
index aefb92483..d0ae225de 100644
--- a/kernel/src/arch/x86_64/trap.s
+++ b/kernel/src/arch/x86_64/trap.s
@@ -491,7 +491,7 @@ isr_paranoid:
     or rax, rdx
     mov rbx, rax                    # the GS base we found, given back on the way out
     cmp rbx, [rsp + 176]
-    je 1f
+    jmp 1f
     mov rax, [rsp + 176]            # this core's per-CPU pointer
     mov rdx, rax
     shr rdx, 32
